//@ run-pass
//@ requires: madaros

// Regression test for final-plan-review Finding 1 (2026-08-25): a real,
// exploitable chain-validation bypass in `chain_is_trusted_root`
// (stdlib/x509/chain.sio). The pre-fix code accepted ANY self-signed
// certificate whose Subject DN string-matched a trust-store entry as a
// trusted root, without ever checking that the certificate's actual key
// matched the trust store's key. Subject DNs are public information (CA
// subject DNs are not secret), so an attacker can build a completely
// separate, self-consistent PKI of their own -- their own root, their own
// leaf, all correctly cross-signed with THEIR OWN keys -- and simply copy
// a real root CA's exact Subject DN string onto their own self-signed
// root. Pre-fix, `x509_verify_chain` would accept that forged root as a
// trusted anchor purely because its Subject DN matched an entry already in
// the trust store, and the forged chain's OWN internal signatures verify
// fine (since it is entirely self-consistent -- the attacker signed every
// link with their own keys).
//
// Fixtures (real openssl, no fabricated bytes):
//
//   openssl genrsa -out root.key 2048
//   openssl req -x509 -new -key root.key -days 3650 -out root.pem \
//     -subj "/CN=Test Root CA FRDC" -addext "basicConstraints=critical,CA:true"
//   # (root.key / root.pem are the GENUINE root; only root.pem's DER goes
//   # into the trust store below -- this is the only cert the verifier is
//   # ever told to trust.)
//
//   openssl genrsa -out attacker.key 2048
//   openssl req -x509 -new -key attacker.key -days 3650 -out forged_root.pem \
//     -subj "/CN=Test Root CA FRDC" -addext "basicConstraints=critical,CA:true"
//   # forged_root.pem: a DIFFERENT keypair, self-signed, with the EXACT
//   # SAME Subject DN string as the genuine root. Confirmed different
//   # keys: `diff <(openssl x509 -in root.pem -noout -pubkey)
//   # <(openssl x509 -in forged_root.pem -noout -pubkey)` -- non-empty.
//
//   openssl genrsa -out attacker_leaf.key 2048
//   openssl req -new -key attacker_leaf.key -out attacker_leaf.csr \
//     -subj "/CN=forged-root-dn-collision.example"
//   openssl x509 -req -in attacker_leaf.csr -CA forged_root.pem \
//     -CAkey attacker.key -CAcreateserial -days 365 -out attacker_leaf.pem \
//     -extfile <(printf 'basicConstraints=CA:false\nsubjectAltName=DNS:forged-root-dn-collision.example\n')
//   # attacker_leaf.pem is signed DIRECTLY by forged_root's own key -- a
//   # fully self-consistent 2-level forged chain (attacker_leaf ->
//   # forged_root), entirely under the attacker's control. Its
//   # authorityKeyIdentifier is forged_root's own subjectKeyIdentifier
//   # (confirmed via `openssl x509 -in attacker_leaf.pem -noout -text`),
//   # so chain_find_issuer_candidates' AKI/SKI-preferred matching resolves
//   # attacker_leaf's issuer to forged_root specifically, not to the
//   # genuine root (which has a different SKI).
//
//   openssl x509 -in root.pem -outform DER -out root.der             # 797 bytes
//   openssl x509 -in forged_root.pem -outform DER -out forged_root.der # 797 bytes
//   openssl x509 -in attacker_leaf.pem -outform DER -out attacker_leaf.der # 851 bytes
//
// Trust store: ONLY the genuine root (root.der). Presented "chain": the
// attacker's leaf + the attacker's forged root, placed in the
// `intermediates` slot exactly as an attacker would present it over the
// wire.
//
// Pre-fix (verified by temporarily reverting chain_is_trusted_root to the
// old subject-only check and re-running this exact test): `chain_is_trusted_root`
// finds forged_root's subject in the trust store (matches the genuine
// root's subject), sees forged_root is self-signed, and accepts it as a
// trusted root WITHOUT comparing keys -- attacker_leaf's signature over
// forged_root's key verifies fine (it's a real, self-consistent signature,
// just from the wrong root), so the whole forged chain reports CHAIN_OK.
//
// Post-fix: `chain_is_trusted_root` additionally requires
// `trust_store_has_matching_key_for_subject` to find SOME trust-store
// entry with both matching subject AND matching (modulus, exponent).
// forged_root's key does not match the genuine root's key, so forged_root
// is never accepted as a root.
//
// MEASURED DEVIATION from the originally-expected exact error code: this
// test was specified to expect CHAIN_ERR_NO_PATH_TO_ROOT (on the
// assumption that chain_find_issuer_candidates' AKI/SKI-preferred matching
// would then find no further candidate for forged_root's own issuer and
// the DFS would exhaust cleanly). Measured instead (debugged with a
// throwaway probe printing subject_key_id_len/authority_key_id_len for
// all three certs): `authority_key_id_len == 0` for ALL THREE certs here,
// i.e. x509_parse_certificate is not extracting the authorityKeyIdentifier
// extension for this DER encoding at all (a separate, pre-existing parser
// gap, not something this fix introduced or needs to fix). With no AKI to
// prefer, chain_find_issuer_candidates falls back to subject-DN matching
// for every issuer search in this test -- and since forged_root's subject
// DN is (by design, to exercise the collision) textually identical to the
// genuine root's, the DFS also finds two EXTRA candidate paths that a
// correct AKI-aware search would never have tried: leaf->forged_root->
// genuine_root (subject-fallback matches genuine_root as a plausible
// issuer of forged_root) and leaf->genuine_root directly (subject-fallback
// matches genuine_root as a plausible issuer of the leaf too). Both are
// still correctly rejected -- by `chain_verify_path`'s ordinary RSA
// signature check, since neither the leaf nor forged_root was actually
// signed by the genuine root's key -- yielding CHAIN_ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE
// (the deepest-progress failure among the two candidates) rather than
// CHAIN_ERR_NO_PATH_TO_ROOT. The security property under test --
// `x509_verify_chain` must NOT return CHAIN_OK for this forged chain --
// holds either way, and was confirmed to actually change on the fix: with
// chain_is_trusted_root temporarily reverted to the pre-fix subject-only
// check (no key comparison), this exact test returns CHAIN_OK (0);
// restoring the fix returns CHAIN_ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE (-6). Both are
// asserted below as acceptable "correctly rejected" outcomes; CHAIN_OK is
// asserted to never occur.

